## Intro

[Figma](https://www.figma.com) provides a completely free platform for designing user interfaces, allowing anyone to start creating without any licensing costs. The tool runs in a web browser, so there is no need to install heavyweight software, and all projects are stored in the cloud, making them instantly accessible from any device.

Collaboration is built into the core of Figma. Multiple designers can open the same file simultaneously, see each other’s cursors in real time, and leave comments directly on the canvas. This eliminates the back‑and‑forth of exporting assets and ensures that feedback loops are fast and transparent.

Because the service is web‑based, it also includes a mobile companion app. Designers can preview their work on a phone, checking how layouts and interactions translate to smaller screens. This on‑the‑go testing helps catch responsiveness issues early in the design process.

The interface is intentionally simple: a toolbar on the left provides frames, shapes, and text tools, while the right‑hand panel houses properties such as colors, typography, and effects. Users can drag and drop images, adjust corner radii, and edit text in place, all without leaving the browser.

Figma’s free tier removes barriers for beginners and small teams. There are no hidden fees for basic features like version history, sharing, or commenting. This makes it an ideal entry point for students, freelancers, and hobbyists who want to experiment with UI/UX design without financial commitment.

Throughout the tutorial, the workflow is broken down into clear, sequential steps: creating an account, setting up frames for websites or apps, applying colors and typography, and finally sharing or exporting the design. Each stage demonstrates how the collaborative environment streamlines typical design tasks.

By the end of the session, viewers will have seen how Figma’s combination of free access, real‑time collaboration, and cross‑device preview creates a powerful, low‑friction workflow for building modern UI/UX designs.

## Create an account on Figma 00:50

Navigate to the Figma landing page and click the **Get started** button. This action opens the sign‑up interface where two registration options appear: **Continue with Google** and the traditional email form.

Select Continue with Google to use a Google identity for authentication. A pop‑up lists the Google accounts currently logged into the browser; choose the desired account and then press **Next** to confirm the selection.

If the Google flow is preferred, the system automatically imports the account’s name and profile picture. No manual entry of name or password is required, streamlining the onboarding experience.

After the Google account is confirmed, Figma presents an optional invitation screen. Here you may add email addresses of future collaborators, but this step can be skipped to create the account immediately.

Proceed by clicking **Skip** on the questionnaire that follows. The questionnaire asks about design experience and preferred tools, but none of the answers affect the creation of a free account.

Finalize the registration by pressing **Start for free**. Figma then redirects to the personal dashboard, confirming that the account has been successfully provisioned.

The dashboard displays a welcome panel and a **Create new file** button. At this point the user has full access to Figma’s free tier, which includes unlimited files, real‑time collaboration, and the ability to publish designs to the Community.

To begin a design project, click the **Design file** tile on the dashboard. This opens the main editing canvas where frames, components, and other design elements can be added.

If the email sign‑up route is preferred, click the **Sign up with email** link on the same page. Enter a full name, a valid email address, and a secure password, then press **Create account**. The same optional questionnaire appears and can be skipped in the same manner.

Regardless of the chosen method, the resulting account is a free Figma account that provides immediate access to all core design tools without any credit‑card information or subscription commitment.

## Create a UI design for a website 01:40

Open the Figma dashboard, click Design file, and the editor canvas appears. This workspace is where every visual element lives; the left panel lists pages and layers, while the right panel holds properties for the selected object. Starting from a clean canvas ensures that no hidden elements interfere with the layout.

Select the **Frame** tool from the top toolbar and choose the **Desktop** preset. The preset inserts a frame sized for typical desktop resolutions (1440 × 1024 px). Using a predefined desktop frame gives a reliable grid and pixel density, which helps maintain consistency when the design is later exported or handed off to developers.

If the default size does not match the target device, resize the frame by dragging any corner while holding **Shift** to preserve the aspect ratio. Keeping the frame dimensions aligned with the intended viewport prevents unexpected scrollbars and makes responsive breakpoints easier to calculate later in the workflow.

### Building the Header Section

Create a new section for navigation by selecting the **Rectangle** tool, drawing a horizontal band across the top of the frame, and naming the layer **Header** in the layers panel. The header acts as a visual anchor; placing it first establishes a clear hierarchy and provides a reference point for aligning subsequent elements.

Change the header’s background color: with the rectangle selected, open the **Fill** picker on the right, click the color swatch, and pick a hue that reflects the brand identity. Adjust the opacity slider if a translucent overlay is desired, which can add depth when a background image is placed behind the header.

Add the site title using the **Text** tool. Click inside the header rectangle, type the desired name (for example, “TravelCo”), then use the properties panel to set the font family, weight, and size. Larger, bold text draws immediate attention, signaling the primary brand element to users.

Create navigation links by duplicating the title text layer (Ctrl + D), editing the copy to read **Home**, **About**, **Services**, and **Contact**, and spacing them evenly across the header. Align the links using Figma’s smart guides; when a layer snaps to the center line, the guide appears, guaranteeing consistent horizontal distribution.

### Designing the Hero Area

Below the header, draw another full‑width rectangle to serve as the hero section. Apply a contrasting fill—either a solid color or a gradient—to differentiate it from the header. The hero area typically captures the visitor’s attention, so a bold background helps the headline stand out.

Insert a headline inside the hero rectangle with the Text tool. Choose a large, bold typeface (e.g., 48 pt) and type a compelling message such as “Explore the World with Ease”. Larger font sizes create visual hierarchy, making the headline the focal point of the page.

Add a sub‑headline beneath the main headline using a smaller font weight (e.g., 24 pt). This secondary text provides supporting information and should be legible without competing with the primary headline. Adjust line height in the properties panel to improve readability.

Drag an image file from the computer onto the canvas to populate the hero background. Drop the file directly onto the hero rectangle; Figma automatically creates an image layer that inherits the rectangle’s dimensions. This drag‑and‑drop workflow eliminates the need for separate import dialogs and speeds up iteration.

Resize the image by selecting its corners and holding Shift to keep the aspect ratio intact. Position the image so that the most important visual elements remain visible—use the alignment guides that appear when the image edges line up with the rectangle’s edges. Proper positioning ensures that the hero remains visually balanced on different screen sizes.

### Adding a Services Section

Create a new section beneath the hero by drawing a series of equal‑width rectangles that act as columns. Each column will host an icon or image and a short description. Using columns provides a modular structure that can be rearranged easily if the content changes.

For each column, drag an icon or illustrative image onto the canvas, then resize it to fit within the column’s bounds. Pair the visual with a text block that briefly describes the service (e.g., “Flight Booking”). The combination of image and text reinforces the message and aids skimmability.

### Constructing the Footer

At the bottom of the frame, draw a thin rectangle to serve as the footer. Fill it with a dark color to create contrast with the rest of the page. Insert small‑print text for copyright information and secondary navigation links (e.g., “Privacy Policy”, “Terms of Service”). Align the footer elements using the same smart‑guide system employed earlier, guaranteeing consistent margins.

### Refining Layout and Consistency

Toggle **Outline** mode (Ctrl + Y) to view only the structural outlines of all layers. This view helps spot misaligned elements, uneven spacing, or stray objects that might have been left behind. Adjust margins, padding, and alignment until the design exhibits a clean, grid‑based rhythm, which developers can later translate into CSS grid or flexbox layouts.

### Why These Steps Matter

Selecting the desktop frame first anchors the design to a concrete pixel canvas, preventing ambiguous scaling later. Creating distinct sections (header, hero, services, footer) establishes a visual hierarchy that guides user attention and improves usability. Drag‑and‑drop image insertion streamlines asset placement, while using Figma’s built‑in alignment tools ensures pixel‑perfect spacing—critical for handoff to developers who rely on exact measurements.

## Create a UI design For an app 03:30

Open the **Frames** panel and switch to the **Phone** category. Select the **iPhone 13 mini** preset; the canvas instantly displays the device outline with its native screen dimensions. This frame provides the correct pixel density and safe‑area margins, ensuring that UI elements will align with real‑world iPhone screens.

Treat the iPhone frame like any other canvas. Use the Rectangle tool to lay out a header bar, then apply a fill color from the right‑hand properties panel. Drag a text box onto the header, type a title such as “Home,” and adjust the font size and weight to match typical mobile typography.

Add a navigation bar at the bottom by drawing another rectangle and setting its height to roughly 56 px, which matches iOS guidelines. Populate the bar with icons—either drawn directly with the **Pen** tool or imported from a vector library—then align them using the built‑in alignment shortcuts.

Create a content area by inserting an image placeholder. Drag an image file from the desktop onto the canvas; Figma automatically creates a mask that fits the frame. Resize the image to fill the width of the screen while preserving its aspect ratio, then add a subtle drop shadow in the effects section for visual depth.

Overlay textual information on the image using the Text tool. Stack a headline, sub‑headline, and body copy vertically, then use the spacing controls to achieve consistent line height. Apply a semi‑transparent background rectangle behind the text to improve readability against the image.

Insert a primary call‑to‑action button beneath the image. Draw a rounded rectangle, set its corner radius to 8 px, and fill it with a contrasting accent color. Add a text label such as “Book Now,” center it horizontally and vertically, and use the **Auto Layout** feature to keep the label centered if the button size changes.

If a faster start is desired, remember that a template can be imported later. The workflow for a custom app screen mirrors the website process: select a frame, place shapes, add text, import images, and style components. By mastering these core tools on the iPhone 13 mini frame, the same techniques can be reused for any mobile device.

## Choose a template for your UI design 03:52

### Choosing a Template for Your UI Design

Navigate back to the **Files** view in the left‑hand sidebar. This view lists all of the design files you own and provides quick access to Figma’s shared resource hub. Click the **Explore Community** button located at the top of the panel. The Community window opens, presenting a searchable catalog of UI kits, icon packs, and full‑screen templates contributed by designers worldwide.

The Community search bar functions like any standard search field: type the keyword that describes the type of app you are building—in this case, **travel app**—and press **Enter**. Figma instantly filters the catalog and displays a grid of matching templates. Each result shows a thumbnail preview, the template’s title, and a short description that hints at the screens and components included.

Scanning the results, look for a template whose visual style aligns with the brand you plan to create. For example, a template might feature a clean, white background with bold accent colors, while another might use a darker theme with high‑contrast imagery. Selecting a thumbnail opens the template’s detail page, where a larger preview reveals the layout of each screen, the color palette, and the hierarchy of components.

When you have identified a template that satisfies the project’s aesthetic and functional needs, click the **Get a copy** button located on the right side of the detail page. This action creates a personal duplicate of the template inside your own workspace, leaving the original file untouched in the Community for other users to explore.

After the copy is created, the new file appears at the top of your **Recent files** list. Open the file to confirm that all layers, components, and pages have been transferred correctly. The file retains the original name—such as “Travel App UI Kit”—which you can rename by double‑clicking the file name in the file list and typing a new title that reflects your project’s identity.

Inspect the structure of the copied template using the **Layers** panel on the left. Notice how the original designer grouped related screens into separate frames (e.g., Home, Search Results, Detail View) and organized reusable elements into component sets (buttons, navigation bars, cards). Consistent naming conventions like `Header/Logo` or `Button/Primary` make it easy to locate and modify specific parts later on.

Identify the primary artboards that represent the core user flows of a travel app. Typically, these include a home screen that showcases featured destinations, a search results screen that lists available trips, and a detail screen that provides in‑depth information about a selected package. These frames serve as the scaffolding for your custom content and branding.

Before you begin any modifications, duplicate the main frames to preserve the untouched original layout. Select a frame, then press **Ctrl +D** (or **Cmd +D** on macOS) to create an exact copy. Rename the duplicate to something descriptive like “Home – Custom” so you can easily differentiate between the source and the version you will edit.

With duplicated frames in place, you can start replacing placeholder assets. Drag a new background image from your computer onto the canvas; Figma automatically creates an image layer that snaps to the frame’s bounds. Because the template’s layers are already organized, the new image inherits the same constraints, ensuring it scales correctly across different device sizes.

Text elements in the template are editable directly on the canvas. Double‑click any text block to enter edit mode, then replace generic copy such as “Explore the world” with your own messaging. Adjust the font size, weight, or family using the right‑hand properties panel to match your brand’s typographic guidelines. Because the template uses text styles, changing a style updates all instances of that style throughout the file.

Color adjustments are performed by selecting a component and clicking the color swatch in the properties panel. The template may already contain a set of color styles (e.g., `Primary/Blue`, `Background/Light`). To adopt your brand colors, edit these styles: click the style name, choose **Edit**, and input your hexadecimal values. All components linked to the style automatically reflect the new colors, saving you from manually recoloring each element.

The advantage of using a Community template lies in the pre‑built component architecture. Buttons, input fields, and navigation bars are already defined as components with variants for different states (default, hover, pressed). When you need a new button, simply duplicate the existing component and modify its label; the interaction states remain intact, ensuring consistent behavior across the app.

Finally, verify that the duplicated and edited frames still maintain proper layer hierarchy. Use the Outline view to collapse groups and confirm that no stray layers are left outside their intended groups. A clean hierarchy prevents accidental edits and makes handoff to developers smoother, as they can quickly locate the relevant symbols and export assets.

By following these steps—searching the Community, selecting a travel‑app template, copying it into your workspace, and preparing duplicated frames for customization—you establish a solid foundation for the next phase of the workflow: editing the UI to reflect your unique brand and content. This approach accelerates design time, leverages proven UI patterns, and ensures that the file remains well‑organized for future collaboration.

## Edit this UI design. 04:40

### Edit Template Elements

Start editing the copied travel‑app template by double‑clicking any canvas element. Double‑click activates the element’s inline edit mode and opens its property fields on the right‑hand sidebar, eliminating the need to search through menus. This direct interaction speeds up iteration because the designer can see changes in real time while staying focused on the visual layout.

### Replacing the Background Image

To replace the background picture, double‑click the large rectangle that forms the screen’s backdrop. In the sidebar, locate the Fill section; the current image thumbnail appears there. Click the thumbnail to open a small dialog, then choose **Image → Choose**. Navigate to the desired picture on your computer, select it, and confirm. Figma instantly swaps the old asset for the new one, preserving the rectangle’s dimensions and layer order.

The new image may not match the frame’s aspect ratio perfectly. Within the same Fill panel, use the **Fit**, **Crop**, or **Tile** options to control scaling. Fit maintains the image’s original proportions while ensuring the entire picture is visible inside the rectangle. Crop fills the rectangle completely but may cut off edges, which is useful for emphasizing a focal point. Adjusting these settings prevents unwanted stretching and keeps the design crisp.

If the background needs a subtle overlay—common for improving text readability—add a second fill layer. Click **+** under Fill, choose a solid color, and set its opacity to around 30 %. This semi‑transparent layer darkens the image just enough to make white or light‑colored type stand out without obscuring the photo.

### Editing Text Content

Select any text block by double‑clicking it. The cursor appears, allowing you to type new copy directly. Because the text layer remains selected, the sidebar shows typographic controls: **Font family**, **Weight**, **Size**, **Line height**, and **Letter spacing**. Changing the **Font** updates the visual style across the whole block, while adjusting Size and Line height fine‑tunes readability on different screen sizes.

For example, the original template might use a thin 12 pt font for a tagline. If the design calls for stronger hierarchy, increase the size to 16 pt and switch the weight to **Bold**. The live preview updates instantly, letting you compare the impact of each typographic tweak without leaving the canvas.

When the text includes placeholder data (e.g., “Destination Name”), replace it with realistic content to evaluate spacing. Figma automatically expands the text box as you type, but you can also manually set a fixed width to test how longer strings wrap. This practice helps avoid overflow issues later when the app receives real user data.

### Adjusting Button Colors for Contrast

Select the button shape with a single click. In the Fill section, click the color swatch to launch the color picker. Because the background is dark, choose a bright hue—such as a vivid teal or electric blue—to create strong visual contrast. High contrast ensures the call‑to‑action is immediately noticeable, which is essential for usability and accessibility.

If the brand already defines specific colors, enter the exact hex code (e.g., `#00A8E8`) into the picker’s input field. This guarantees color consistency across all screens and aligns the prototype with brand guidelines. Figma stores the hex value, so any future element can reuse it without re‑typing.

To propagate the chosen button color to other interactive elements, use the **Copy/Paste Style** shortcut (Ctrl + Alt + C to copy, Ctrl + Alt + V to paste). This copies the entire style—including fill, stroke, and effects—and applies it to the next selected layer, saving time and reducing manual errors.

### Maintaining Consistency Across the Design

After updating one button, scan the rest of the screen for additional call‑to‑action elements. Select each one and repeat the color change, or apply the copied style as described above. Consistent button styling creates a clear visual language, guiding users toward actionable items without confusion.

If the template contains multiple button states (default, hover, pressed), adjust each state’s fill color accordingly. Figma’s **Component** system lets you edit the master component, automatically updating all instances—a powerful way to keep the UI coherent as you iterate.

### Quick Tips for Efficient Editing

> Tip Use the **Selection colors** panel (found under the eye‑dropper icon) to copy a color from any element and paste it onto another. This is faster than opening the full color picker each time.

> Note Double‑clicking is the fastest way to enter edit mode for both images and text. Avoid using the right‑click context menu unless you need advanced options such as **Detach instance** or **Reset overrides**.

### Final Review Before Moving On

When all edits are complete, switch to **Presentation mode** by pressing **P**. This displays the frame in full‑screen without UI chrome, allowing you to evaluate how the new background, updated copy, and refreshed button colors work together. Check for legibility, contrast, and overall aesthetic balance. If anything feels off—such as text blending into the background—return to the appropriate panel, adjust opacity or color, and re‑preview.

A thorough visual check at this stage prevents the need for later rework once you add branding or begin prototyping, keeping the workflow smooth and efficient.

## Add a brand logo to your design 06:16

Drag the logo file from the local computer directly onto the Figma canvas. The file appears as an image layer positioned where it was dropped, automatically becoming part of the current frame.

Select the newly placed logo layer. Small square handles appear at each corner of the bounding box, indicating that the layer can be resized.

Resize the logo by clicking and dragging a corner handle. Holding Shift while dragging preserves the original aspect ratio, preventing distortion of the brand mark.

Adjust the size until the logo fits comfortably within the intended area of the design. The goal is to make the logo prominent enough to be recognizable but not so large that it overwhelms surrounding content.

If precise dimensions are required, open the right‑hand properties panel after selecting the logo. Enter exact width and height values in pixels; the other dimension updates automatically when the lock icon is active.

Position the logo by dragging the entire layer. Align it with other UI elements using Figma’s smart guides, which appear as temporary lines when the layer is near the center or edges of the frame.

For consistent spacing, use the spacing values shown in the properties panel. Set a uniform margin between the logo and the nearest edge or adjacent component to maintain visual balance.

If the design includes a dark background, consider changing the logo’s color or adding a white overlay. This can be done by selecting the logo layer and applying a fill color adjustment in the properties panel.

When the logo needs to appear on multiple screens, duplicate the layer (Ctrl + D) and place copies in each relevant frame. This ensures brand consistency across the entire prototype.

Group the logo with related elements (Ctrl + G) to keep the layer hierarchy organized. Grouping allows the logo to move together with associated components during later layout tweaks.

After finalizing size and placement, lock the logo layer (Ctrl + L) to prevent accidental movement while editing other parts of the design.

Review the design at actual size (100 % zoom) to confirm that the logo remains crisp and legible. If pixelation is observed, replace the source file with a higher‑resolution version and repeat the drag‑and‑drop step.

With the logo correctly sized and positioned, the design now reflects the brand identity, ready for the next steps such as adding navigation icons or building interactive prototypes.

## Create another frame from scratch 06:40

### Creating the Tour Details Frame

Begin by adding a fresh frame that will host the “Tour Details” screen. Select the Frame tool from the left‑hand toolbar, then choose the **iPhone 13 mini** preset from the dropdown that appears. This preset automatically sets the canvas size to the exact dimensions of an iPhone 13 mini, ensuring that all subsequent elements are sized correctly for a mobile view. After the frame appears on the canvas, rename it for easy identification: double‑click the layer name in the layers panel and type **Tour Details**. Naming layers clearly helps keep the file organized, especially when many frames are present.

The reason for using a preset frame rather than a custom rectangle is that Figma applies device‑specific pixel density and safe‑area guidelines automatically. This means any UI components you place will align with the device’s native resolution, reducing the need for manual adjustments later when the design is handed off to developers.

### Adding and Styling the Image

Next, bring in a hero image that will illustrate the travel package. Drag an image file from the computer directly onto the newly created frame. Figma instantly creates an image layer that can be moved, resized, and masked. Position the image at the top of the frame so it spans the full width, leaving a margin at the bottom for text and the button.

The image serves as a visual anchor; users typically focus on the top portion of a mobile screen first, so a compelling picture captures attention immediately.

To give the image a modern, friendly feel, round its corners. With the image selected, locate the **Corner radius** field in the right‑hand properties panel and type **65**. This value creates a noticeable curve without distorting the image. Rounded corners are a common design pattern in mobile UI because they soften the visual hierarchy and make elements appear more touch‑friendly.

Figma updates the preview in real time, so you can see the effect instantly and adjust the radius if needed.

Sometimes a design calls for a flat top edge while keeping the bottom corners rounded. To achieve this, click the **Independent corners** icon (four separate squares) next to the radius field. This unlocks each corner’s value. Set the top‑left and top‑right radii to **0** while leaving the bottom‑left and bottom‑right at 65. The result is a rectangular image with a flat upper edge and softened lower corners, matching the style used elsewhere in the tutorial.

This technique demonstrates how Figma lets you fine‑tune geometry without needing external image editing tools.

### Inserting Text Content

With the visual foundation in place, add the textual description of the travel package. Select the Text tool (shortcut **T**), click just below the image, and start typing. Include a clear title, a brief tagline, and any essential details such as dates, price, or highlights. For example:

```
Explore the Alps
7‑day adventure, all inclusive
From $1,299 per person
```

Using line breaks creates a hierarchy that guides the eye from the most important information (the title) to supporting details.

The properties panel lets you adjust font family, weight, size, and line height. Choose a legible font size for mobile—typically 16‑18 px for body text and 24‑28 px for headings. Proper typographic scaling ensures readability on small screens and maintains visual rhythm.

Why typography matters: consistent font choices reinforce brand identity, while appropriate line spacing prevents the text from feeling cramped. Figma’s live preview shows exactly how the text will appear on the device, allowing you to iterate quickly.

If the design calls for emphasis, you can bold the title or change its color using the color picker in the right‑hand panel. This visual distinction draws attention to the most critical piece of information—the name of the tour.

### Copying and Customizing the Button

The final interactive element is a call‑to‑action button. Instead of building a new button from scratch, duplicate an existing one to keep styling consistent across the app. Select the original button component, press **Ctrl + C** (or **Cmd + C** on macOS) to copy, then click inside the “Tour Details” frame and press **Ctrl + V** (or **Cmd + V**) to paste. The duplicated button appears directly where you clicked.

Copying preserves properties such as corner radius, shadow, and background color, which speeds up workflow and maintains a cohesive UI language.

Now add a label to the newly pasted button. With the button still selected, activate the Text tool, click on the button surface, and type a concise action phrase like **Book Now**. Adjust the text color to contrast sharply with the button’s background—white text on a dark button, for instance—so the label stands out. Use the properties panel to set the font size (usually 14‑16 px for buttons) and align the text centrally both horizontally and vertically.

The contrast ratio is crucial for accessibility; a high‑contrast label ensures that users with visual impairments can perceive the button’s purpose.

After labeling, verify the button’s interactive affordance. Figma automatically adds a subtle inner shadow to indicate pressability, but you can enhance this by adding a slight drop shadow in the effects section. A shadow of **2 px** offset, **4 px** blur, and 20 % opacity gives the button depth without overwhelming the design.

These visual cues communicate to users that the element is tappable, which is essential for a smooth mobile experience.

### Final Review and Alignment Checks

With the image, text, and button in place, perform a quick alignment audit. Select all three layers (Shift‑click each) and use the **Align horizontal centers** button in the top toolbar to ensure they share the same central axis. Then, distribute vertical spacing evenly by selecting the image and text, clicking **Tidy up** (the auto‑layout shortcut), and adjusting the spacing value to maintain consistent gaps.

Consistent alignment prevents visual clutter and guides the user’s eye naturally from the image to the description and finally to the action button.

Why this matters: well‑aligned components reduce cognitive load, making the interface feel intuitive. Figma’s alignment tools provide pixel‑perfect precision, which is especially important on small screens where every pixel counts.

Once satisfied, lock the frame (right‑click → **Lock**) to avoid accidental moves while you continue working on other parts of the project. Locking also signals to collaborators that this section is finalized.

## Add navigation icon to the design 08:57

### Installing the IconScout Plugin

Open the **Plugins** menu in the top toolbar of Figma and choose **Browse Plugins**. In the search field type “IconScout” and press Enter. The marketplace returns the official IconScout plugin; click **Install** to add it to the current file. Installing the plugin registers it with Figma’s plugin registry, allowing the editor to call the external icon library directly from the canvas without leaving the design environment.

After installation, the plugin appears under **Plugins → IconScout**. Run it immediately by selecting **Run** from the same submenu. Running the plugin opens a floating panel on the right side of the canvas where all subsequent icon‑related actions will take place. This panel acts as a bridge between Figma and the IconScout API, fetching vector icons in real time.

### Logging into IconScout

When the IconScout panel first appears, it prompts for authentication. Click the **Login** button, then choose **Sign up with Google**. The Google OAuth flow opens in a new browser tab; select the desired Google account and grant the requested permissions. IconScout creates a user profile linked to that Google identity, which is why the video shows a quick account creation without manual email entry.

Once the Google sign‑in completes, return to the IconScout panel in Figma and click **Login with Web**. A second dialog asks for **Grant Access**; confirming this step authorizes the current Figma file to retrieve icons from the newly created IconScout account. The grant is necessary because the plugin must read the user’s icon library and download SVG assets on demand.

After granting access, the panel updates to show a search bar and a message confirming that the icon pack is now available. This confirmation indicates that the OAuth token has been stored securely in the Figma session, enabling seamless icon retrieval for the remainder of the design session.

### Finding and Inserting a Back‑Arrow Icon

With authentication complete, click inside the IconScout search field and type “back arrow”. Press Enter to query the IconScout database. The plugin returns a list of vector icons that match the keyword, each displayed with a preview thumbnail. The video selects the first result, which is a simple left‑pointing arrow suitable for navigation.

Select the desired icon by clicking its thumbnail, then press the **Insert** button. Figma instantly places the SVG on the canvas at the location of the mouse cursor. Because the icon is an SVG, it remains fully scalable and editable within Figma, preserving crisp edges at any size.

After insertion, the icon appears near the center of the frame. Drag the icon to the left edge of the navigation bar to emulate a typical back‑navigation control. Positioning the icon manually ensures alignment with other UI elements such as the screen title and any right‑hand actions. The video demonstrates moving the icon by clicking and holding, then dragging it into place.

### Customizing Color and Opacity

Select the newly placed back‑arrow icon to reveal its properties in the right‑hand **Design** panel. Under Fill, click the color swatch to open the color picker. Choose white (hex #FFFFFF) to contrast against the dark background used in the app mockup. Changing the fill updates the SVG’s internal path color, which is why the icon instantly turns white without needing to replace the asset.

Next, adjust the opacity slider located just below the color picker. Drag it to 80 % to give the icon a slightly muted appearance, indicating that it is a secondary navigation element rather than a primary call‑to‑action. Opacity is applied as a CSS‑like alpha channel on the vector, affecting the entire icon uniformly.

For designers who want a hover state, create a component variant. Duplicate the icon (Ctrl + D), change its fill to a lighter shade of white (e.g., 90 % opacity), and name the variant “Hover”. Then, in **Prototype** mode, add an interaction that switches to the hover variant on **On Hover**. This technique provides immediate visual feedback when users move the cursor over the icon during the prototype preview.

### Previewing and Verifying the Navigation Icon

Enter **Presentation** mode by clicking the **Play** button in the top‑right corner of the Figma UI. Use the left and right arrow keys on the keyboard to navigate between frames. Observe that the back‑arrow icon appears on the current frame, retains its white fill, and respects the 80 % opacity setting. The preview confirms that the icon behaves as expected when the prototype is played back.

If additional navigation icons are needed—such as a forward arrow or a menu hamburger—repeat the search‑and‑insert workflow. Consistently apply the same fill color, opacity, and size to each icon to maintain a unified visual language throughout the app. The video notes that the IconScout library contains thousands of icons, making it easy to find matching symbols for any navigation pattern.

Finally, save the file to lock in all changes. Choose **File → Save** or press **Ctrl + S**. Saving writes the updated vector data, plugin metadata, and component variants to the Figma file, ensuring that collaborators who later open the file will see the same navigation icon configuration. This prepares the design for the next stage—creating an interactive prototype that links the back‑arrow to the previous frame.

## Create an interactive prototype of our app. 11:04

### Create an Interactive Prototype

Begin by identifying the UI element that will act as the navigation trigger. In this tutorial the primary button on the home screen is used, but any clickable object—such as a text link or an image—can serve the same purpose. Selecting the element activates the right‑hand panel where the Prototype tab resides, signalling to Figma that the object will participate in interaction flows.

Switch to the Prototype tab. This panel replaces the usual Design settings with interaction controls, allowing you to define what happens when a user engages with the selected element. The tab displays a small circular node attached to the element; this node is the anchor point for drawing connections between frames.

Hover over the circular node until it turns solid, then click and drag a connector line toward the destination frame. In the video the target is the “Tour Details” frame that was created earlier. Release the mouse button when the cursor is over the frame thumbnail; Figma automatically creates a link and draws a thin line indicating the relationship.

A pop‑up window appears immediately after the link is established. The window contains an **Interaction** dropdown, a **Destination** selector, and optional animation settings. By default the dropdown shows **“on click”**, meaning the linked frame will load only when the user clicks the element. This default aligns with typical mobile navigation patterns where taps initiate screen changes.

Confirm that “on click” remains selected. No additional configuration is required for a basic navigation flow, but the dropdown also offers other triggers such as **“while hovering”** or **“after delay”** for more advanced prototypes. Keeping the default ensures a straightforward tap‑to‑navigate experience that matches the design intent of the travel‑app mockup.

Close the interaction panel by clicking the **X** in the upper‑right corner of the popup. The prototype now contains a functional link: clicking the button will transition the user from the home screen to the “Tour Details” screen. This link is stored in the file’s prototype data and will persist across sessions.

Test the newly created interaction by entering Play mode. Click the play icon located in the top‑right corner of the Figma interface. The canvas switches to a preview window that simulates a real device. Click the button in this preview; the app should instantly display the “Tour Details” frame, confirming that the on‑click action works as expected.

To add more depth to the prototype, repeat the linking process for other interactive elements. The tutorial demonstrates linking the main image on the home screen to the same “Tour Details” frame. Select the image, drag its circular node, and connect it to the destination frame just as you did with the button.

When the image link is created, the same interaction popup appears. This time, instead of leaving the default transition, choose an animation to make the navigation feel smoother. Click the **Animation** dropdown and select **“Move in”**. This animation slides the new frame into view from the side, providing visual continuity that mimics a natural page swipe.

The “Move in” animation works by offsetting the incoming frame off‑screen and then translating it onto the canvas over a short duration (usually 300 ms). The effect reduces the abruptness of a hard cut, helping users maintain context as they move between screens. Figma automatically applies the animation once the interaction is triggered.

Return to Play mode and interact with the image. Observe that the transition now includes the sliding motion rather than an instant jump. This visual cue reinforces the relationship between the home screen and the detail view, making the prototype feel more like a real app.

For consistency, apply the same animation to any other navigation elements that lead to the same destination. Uniform animation styles prevent jarring shifts and give the prototype a cohesive interaction language. If different navigation paths require distinct motions—such as a fade for modal dialogs—select the appropriate animation from the dropdown each time.

After all desired links and animations are in place, review the prototype flow by navigating through each screen in Play mode. Verify that every button and image responds correctly, that the on‑click actions fire, and that the chosen animations play smoothly. This iterative testing helps catch missed connections before sharing the prototype with stakeholders.

When the prototype behaves as intended, save the file. Figma automatically records all interaction data, so no separate export step is needed for the prototype itself. The file can now be shared, presented, or further refined without losing any of the linking or animation settings.

### Why Prototyping Works in Figma

Figma treats each frame as an independent screen while maintaining a global interaction graph. When a connector is drawn between frames, Figma stores a reference to the destination frame ID and the trigger type (e.g., on click). During preview, the engine reads this graph and dynamically swaps the visible canvas, which is why the prototype feels responsive without any code.

Animations such as “Move in” are implemented as CSS‑like transitions applied to the incoming frame. The engine calculates the start and end positions based on the selected direction and interpolates the movement over the defined duration. Because the animation runs client‑side in the browser, it remains smooth even on modest hardware.

Using the Prototype tab separates interaction logic from visual design, allowing designers to experiment with navigation flows without altering the underlying layout. This separation mirrors the principle of separation of concerns in software engineering, making the design file easier to maintain and hand off to developers.

## Share the design with anyone we want. 13:16

### Share the Design

Open the design file and click the **Share** button in the top‑right corner. This opens the sharing dialog where collaborators can be added.

Enter the email address of the person you want to invite in the **Invite people** field. Multiple addresses can be added separated by commas.

Adjust the permission level for each invitee. Selecting Edit grants full editing rights, while **View** restricts the collaborator to read‑only access.

After setting the desired permission, click **Send Invite**. Figma immediately queues an email invitation to the specified addresses.

The invited collaborator receives an email from Figma with a button labeled **Open in Figma**. Clicking this button opens the shared file in the recipient’s browser or desktop app.

Once the file is open, the collaborator’s cursor appears on the canvas, allowing real‑time visual feedback of their presence.

Collaborators can add comments by selecting any element and pressing the **Comment** tool. Their notes appear in the right‑hand panel and are linked to the specific layer.

Project owners can reply to comments directly in the panel, creating a threaded discussion that stays attached to the design context.

To change an existing collaborator’s permission, reopen the Share dialog, locate the user in the list, and switch the dropdown between View and Edit as needed.

If a collaborator should no longer have access, click the **Remove** (X) icon next to their name in the sharing panel. This instantly revokes their ability to view or edit the file.

All comment activity is logged in the **Comments** pane, which can be filtered by resolved or unresolved status, helping teams keep track of feedback cycles.

For larger teams, consider using the **Team** sharing option, which generates a shareable link that can be set to **Anyone with the link can view** or **can edit**, streamlining access without entering individual emails.

Remember to periodically review the sharing settings to ensure that only intended participants retain access, especially before exporting or presenting the final design.

## Download the design to your computer. 14:17

### Exporting Frames as JPGs

Select the design file in the layers panel, then click **Export** in the right‑hand sidebar. A dialog appears where the default format is PNG; change it to **JPG** using the dropdown. Adjust the quality slider if needed, then press Export to save the selected frame to the local downloads folder.

To export multiple frames at once, hold Shift and click each frame, or press **Ctrl+A** to select all frames in the file. After selection, the Export panel shows a combined count; ensure the format is set to JPG for the batch. Clicking Export now generates a single ZIP archive containing every chosen frame as an individual JPG image.

If only a single frame is needed, double‑click its name to isolate it, then repeat the Export steps. The resulting JPG retains the frame’s dimensions and any applied effects, making it ready for presentation or hand‑off to developers.

### Downloading the ZIP Archive

After confirming the export settings, the browser prompts a download of a **ZIP** file. This archive bundles all exported JPGs, preserving the original frame order. Locate the ZIP in the default downloads directory and extract it using any standard archive utility.

Extracted files can be opened directly in image viewers or imported into other design tools. Because each frame is saved as a separate JPG, collaborators can reference individual screens without opening the full Figma file.

For future revisions, repeat the export process after making changes to ensure the ZIP contains the latest visuals. Keeping a consistent naming convention for frames (e.g., “HomeScreen”, “DetailView”) simplifies locating specific assets after extraction.

## Share the design on your phone 14:50

Open the **Figma** file on a desktop and locate the Share button in the top‑right corner. Click Share, then copy the share link that appears. Launch the Figma mobile companion app on the phone, tap **Open link**, and paste the copied URL. The app will load the current file, allowing you to swipe through frames just as you would in the desktop preview. This quick handoff lets you verify layout, spacing, and typography on a real device screen without exporting any assets.

While the design is open in the mobile app, use pinch‑to‑zoom to examine fine details such as icon clarity and text legibility. If any element appears misaligned or too small, return to the desktop version, adjust the component, and the changes will sync automatically to the phone. This live synchronization ensures that the design you see on the phone always reflects the latest edits made in the main file.

To test interactive prototypes on the phone, tap the Play icon in the mobile app’s toolbar. The prototype runs using the same interaction settings defined in the desktop Prototype tab, so on‑click actions, transitions, and animations behave identically. Navigate through the flow by tapping buttons or images, confirming that navigation feels natural on a touch interface. If an animation looks off‑center, adjust the easing or direction in the desktop file and observe the updated behavior instantly on the device.

When sharing the design with stakeholders, generate a **view‑only** link in the desktop Share dialog and send it via email or messaging. Recipients can open the link directly in the mobile app, view the design, and leave comments using the built‑in comment tool. Comments appear as speech‑bubble icons on the canvas; tap a bubble to read the feedback and reply if needed. This streamlined feedback loop eliminates the need for screenshots or separate PDF exports.

> Tip Use the mobile app’s dark‑mode setting to preview how the design looks under different system themes. Switching between light and dark modes in the phone’s settings instantly updates the canvas, revealing any color contrast issues that might not be obvious on a bright desktop monitor.

